    "collected_at": "2026-11-27T22:26:50.204272+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/27/vw-id-buzz-beach-ev-camper-goes-on-sale-starting-over-70000/",
    "canonical_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/27/vw-id-buzz-beach-ev-camper-goes-on-sale-starting-over-70000/",
    "origin_publisher": "Electrek",
    "published_at": "2026-11-27T20:28:58+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-27T20:28:58+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-27T22:27:58.201305+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-27T22:27:58.201305+00:00",
    "body": "Volkswagen opened orders for the new ID. Buzz Beach in the UK , an electric van built for camping and overnight stays, with a fold-out mattress and more. It’s not exactly cheap, though, starting at over $70,000.\nMeet the Volkswagen ID. Buzz Beach\nVolkswagen’s original microbus became a cultural icon thanks to its open, customizable interior, making it perfect for large families, road trips, camping, and, of course, music festivals.\nThe ID. Buzz brings the classic Type 2 microbus into the electric era with a retro-new style, modern tech, and a flexible, spacious cabin.\nBuilding on its reputation as a symbol of freedom and adventure, Volkswagen is adding a new member to the electric people mover lineup.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nThe new ID. Buzz Beach “brings adventure into the electric age,” Volkswagen said after introducing the new variant on Thursday. Designed for camping, longer road trips, and overnight stays, the electric van will be available in 5-, 6-, and 7-seat configurations.\nVolkswagen said it sleeps two comfortably with a removable bed frame and a custom-fitting folding mattress.\nVolkswagen ID. Buzz Beach fold-out mattress (Source: VW UK)\nThe mattress is a decent size at about 6.5 feet long by 4 feet wide (2.0m x 1.2m), roughly the size of an average small double bed.\nIn the 5-seat configuration, the bed frame tucks away in the trunk, while in the 6- and 7-seat layouts, it’s stored vertically behind the third row of seats.\nFor camping and other longer stays, Volkswagen added a new Overnight mode that uses the vehicle’s battery to provide up to 48 hours of continuous, off-grid climate control. The new mode can be accessed on the center touchscreen.\nIt also includes blackout curtains throughout the cabin and ventilation grilles on the front side windows.\nThe ID. Buzz Beach offers vehicle-to-load (V2L) capabilities. A 230V power socket adaptor comes standard, letting you power an e-bike, charge a smartphone, run camping equipment, or power other external electronic devices. A camping connector is available as an added option.\nTo top it off, Volkswagen added a hidden foldable table and chairs set that can be tucked away without taking up internal space.\nVolkswagen opened orders for the new ID. Buzz Beach in the UK this week, starting at £53,955 (excluding VAT), or about $71,000. Including VAT and OTR costs, it starts at £65,837, or roughly $86,500.\nVolkswagen Commercial Vehicles is offering a few financing deals to get you started, with prices from £555 ($726) a month, including a £6,500 ($8,500) deposit.\nVolkswagen ID. Buzz Beach Trim | Starting price excluding VAT | Starting price including VAT and OTR costs\nID. Buzz Beach SWB 286 PS 79 kWh Pro Electric 1-Speed Automatic RWD 5-seater | £53,955 ($70,600) | £65,837 ($86,200)\nID. Buzz Beach SWB 286 PS 79 kWh Pro Electric 1-Speed Automatic RWD 6-seater | £54,085 ($70,800) | £65,993 ($86,400)\nID. Buzz Beach LWB 286 PS 86 kWh Pro Electric 1-Speed Automatic RWD 6-seater | £54,265 ($71,000) | £66,209 ($86,700)\nID. Buzz Beach LWB 286 PS 86 kWh Pro Electric 1-Speed Automatic RWD 7-seater | £54,395 ($71,200) | £66,365 ($86,900)\nVolkswagen ID. Buzz Beach prices by trim in the UK\nWhat about those in the US? Will Volkswagen bring the electric camper to the States?\nTop comment by ModernMarvalFan\nLiked by 9 people\nNice product at the wrong price. If VW can't lower the price because of the cost, then it has a lot more work to do to compete in this market.\nView all comments\nVW introduced the 2027 ID. Buzz with a new Tourer model that was essentially the same as the Beach variant in the UK, with a fold-out mattress, Overnight mode, blackout curtains, and a table and chairs set.\nHowever, VW told its US dealers in September that it would be delaying the ID. Buzz’s return until the 2028 model year.\nThe 2025 Volkswagen ID. Buzz started at about $60,000 in the US, which is one of the biggest reasons it failed to catch on with buyers. The original VW microbus was an affordable people mover.\nA custom camper variant would likely cost more than $70,000. Would you pay $70,000 or more for Volkswagen’s electric camper van?\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
